-- js-globals.rofl — THE ES GLOBALS, WHICH IS THE HALF OF THE STANDARD LIBRARY
-- THAT IS NOT A PROTOTYPE.
--
-- rules/js-env-api.rofl attributes `array.at` and `string.replaceAll` — a
-- member call whose RECEIVER has a known prototype. It says nothing about
-- `JSON.parse`, `Promise.all`, `Object.entries` or `Math.max`, because those
-- hang off a GLOBAL BINDING and a binding is not a receiver. Measured
-- 2026-09-09 over the five files of test/fixtures/js-call: ten stdlib calls,
-- ten attributed, and NOT ONE static of an ES global anywhere in the corpus.
-- The era question could not be asked of `JSON.parse` and nothing said so.
--
-- THREE SURFACES, THREE RELATIONS, AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME QUESTION. This is
-- the decision the pack is built on and it is stated rather than implied:
--
--   the BINDING     does the name `Promise` exist, and since when
--                     -> `es_global[code](E, Name, Release, Form)`
--   the STATICS     what hangs off the NAME: `Promise.all`, `Math.max`
--                     -> `es_static[code](N, Name, Key, Release)`
--   the PROTOTYPE   what hangs off an INSTANCE: `array.at`, `string.concat`
--                     -> `lib_call[code]`, in rules/js-env-api.rofl, keyed by a
--                        lower-case prototype atom. `lib_global_prototype` is
--                        the bridge, and it covers EIGHT of the forty-seven
--                        globals because eight is what `kind_prototype` in
--                        rules/js-dataflow.rofl can name a receiver for.
--                        `new Map()` reaches no prototype at all: there is no
--                        `kind_prototype` arm for a `new_expression`, so a call
--                        on a Map instance is outside both halves and the
--                        missing bridge row is where that is visible.
--
-- AND THE FORMS ARE NOT UNIFORM EITHER. `lib_global_form` has four rows because
-- TypeScript's declarations have four shapes, and averaging them would make
-- three of the four wrong: `Math` and `JSON` are namespace objects with no
-- constructor and no instance surface, `Reflect` and `Intl` are declared as
-- namespace BLOCKS with no variable at all, and `NaN` and `Infinity` are values
-- with no member surface whatever. `es_construct_not_constructor[audit]` below
-- is that difference used rather than merely recorded.

-- ===========================================================================
-- 1. WHERE A GLOBAL CAN BE SEEN, AND THE CEILING IS THIS TABLE.
--
-- A global is an identifier that nothing binds, and "an identifier" is the
-- expensive half: every property name, every declaration id, every object key
-- is an `identifier` node too, and none of them is a reference. Rather than
-- subtract the non-reference positions — a list nobody could close — this names
-- the reference positions the three surfaces actually need, as a table.
--
-- WHAT THAT COSTS, SAID PLAINLY: a bare reference is invisible here. `n > 0 ? n
-- : Infinity` and `typeof Promise` mention a global and neither is the object
-- of a member expression nor a callee, so no row fires.
-- test/js-globals.test.ts asserts that silence BY NAME rather than leaving it
-- to be discovered.
edb(global_ref_position).   -- global_ref_position(Kind, Field)
global_ref_position(member_expression, object).
global_ref_position(optional_member_expression, object).
global_ref_position(call_expression, callee).
global_ref_position(optional_call_expression, callee).
global_ref_position(new_expression, callee).

-- THE SMALL TABLE LEADS, which is a cost decision and the one this layer has
-- paid for twice: `ident_in[code]` exists in rules/js-dataflow.rofl precisely
-- because eight rules led with an unbound `ast_node(E, identifier, _, _)` and
-- handed out 39% of the fixpoint's rows. Five positions x the nodes of those
-- kinds is a selective question; identifiers first would be a scan.
global_ref[code](E, Name, File) :- global_ref_position(K, Field),
                                   ast_node[code](P, K, _, _),
                                   ast_child[code](P, Field, 0, E),
                                   ident_in[code](E, Name, File).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 2. WHAT THE FILE BINDS — AND IT IS DELIBERATELY OVER-BROAD.
--
-- `sees_binder[code]` is the model's real scope relation and it is NOT what
-- this needs: it covers declarators and destructuring patterns only, so a
-- `function Box() {}` or a `class Vat {}` is invisible to it and every call to
-- `Box` would read as a reference to a global named Box. What this question
-- needs is the SAFE direction — a name that anything in the file declares is
-- not a global — and that is a different relation with a different failure
-- mode, so it is written rather than borrowed.
--
-- THE OVER-APPROXIMATION, STATED: FILE-SCOPED, and a name declared anywhere in
-- the file is treated as bound everywhere in it. So a genuine use of the global
-- `Set` in a file that also has a local `Set` is LOST, and a global is never
-- INVENTED. That is the direction an era gate and an effect layer can both
-- live with: this relation under-reports globals and never over-reports them.
edb(declaring_position).   -- declaring_position(Kind, Field)
declaring_position(variable_declarator, id).
declaring_position(function_declaration, id).
declaring_position(class_declaration, id).
declaring_position(function_expression, id).
declaring_position(class_expression, id).
declaring_position(import_specifier, local).
declaring_position(import_default_specifier, local).
declaring_position(import_namespace_specifier, local).
declaring_position(catch_clause, param).

declares_name[code](Name, File) :- declaring_position(K, Field),
                                   ast_node[code](D, K, File, _),
                                   ast_child[code](D, Field, 0, I), ast_name[code](I, Name).
-- ...AND THE NAMES A PATTERN INTRODUCES, which the arm above cannot reach
-- because a declarator whose `id` is an `object_pattern` has no `ast_name` of
-- its own — the same fact that made `destructures[code]` necessary in
-- rules/js-dataflow.rofl. Every name UNDER the binding position counts, keys
-- and defaults included, which widens `declares_name` and therefore narrows
-- the globals: the safe direction again.
declares_name[code](Name, File) :- declaring_position(K, Field),
                                   ast_node[code](D, K, File, _),
                                   ast_child[code](D, Field, 0, I),
                                   ast_within[code](I, X), ast_name[code](X, Name).
-- ...and a PARAMETER, which is a position rather than a declaration kind and so
-- cannot be a row in the table above.
declares_name[code](Name, File) :- fn_node[code](F), fn_file[code](F, File),
                                   ast_child[code](F, params, _, P), ast_name[code](P, Name).
declares_name[code](Name, File) :- fn_node[code](F), fn_file[code](F, File),
                                   ast_child[code](F, params, _, P),
                                   ast_within[code](P, X), ast_name[code](X, Name).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 3. A GLOBAL, AS A FACT RATHER THAN AS A SPELLING.
--
-- THIS IS THE RELATION THE EFFECT LAYER JOINS AGAINST, and it is written here
-- for that as much as for the era question. `layer(effect)` cannot derive `io`
-- for `console.log` from the letters `c-o-n-s-o-l-e`; it needs a row saying
-- that this node is a reference to a global named `console` that nothing in the
-- program binds. That row is `free_global[code]`, and the two relations under
-- it split the world in the way the effect lattice needs:
--
--   `es_global[code](E, Name, Rel, Form)`   ECMAScript declares it. A pure
--                                           language surface, dated.
--   `global_unattributed[audit](E, Name)`   it does not. `console`, `process`,
--                                           `fetch`, `require`, `globalThis` —
--                                           the RUNTIME half, and this audit is
--                                           its in-tray rather than a defect.
--
-- Both are keyed by NODE, so an effect rule joins them to `callee_of[code]`,
-- `arg_at[flow]` or `nearest_fn[code]` without re-deriving anything, and both
-- are keyed by NAME, so a runtime table keyed by name joins straight on.
free_global[code](E, Name, File) :- global_ref[code](E, Name, File),
                                    not declares_name[code](Name, File).

es_global[code](E, Name, Rel, Form) :- free_global[code](E, Name, _),
                                       lib_global(Name, Rel, Form).

global_unattributed[audit](E, Name) :- free_global[code](E, Name, _),
                                       not lib_global(Name, _, _).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 4. THE STATIC SURFACE.
--
-- One join, and it is one join for the same reason `lib_call[code]` is: the
-- corpus side is keyed by (global, key) once `selects[flow]` has answered the
-- key, and so is the library. `selects[flow]` collapses static and computed
-- access, which is right here — `Math["max"]` is the same question as
-- `Math.max`.
es_static[code](N, Name, Key, Rel) :- es_global[code](O, Name, _, _),
                                      ast_child[code](N, object, 0, O),
                                      selects[flow](N, Key), lib_static(Name, Key, Rel).

-- ...AND WHAT IT DOES NOT ATTRIBUTE, which is the row worth watching and the
-- mirror of `stdlib_unattributed[audit]`. A member selected off a KNOWN ES
-- global whose name TypeScript's declarations do not carry is one of three
-- things and this does not guess which: a member of a newer edition than this
-- TypeScript ships, a HOST extension of a language global —
-- `Error.captureStackTrace` is V8's and lives in @types/node — or a typo. It is
-- NOT empty and is not meant to be: the fixture puts a site in it on purpose,
-- because an audit with no site is a claim nothing can falsify.
es_static_unattributed[audit](N, Name, Key) :- es_global[code](O, Name, _, _),
                                               ast_child[code](N, object, 0, O),
                                               selects[flow](N, Key),
                                               not lib_static(Name, Key, _).

-- A WELL-KNOWN SYMBOL IS A KEY AND NOT A CALL, taken from the source rather
-- than asserted: `Symbol.iterator` is a PropertySignature in TypeScript's
-- declaration and `Symbol.for` is a MethodSignature, so `lib_static_shape`
-- already carries the difference and this reads it. `Math.PI` and
-- `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` come out the same way, by the same rule, for free.
es_static_key[code](N, Name, Key) :- es_static[code](N, Name, Key, _),
                                     lib_static_shape(Name, Key, data).

-- WHAT IS DONE WITH THE BINDING ITSELF, and the two are different facts about
-- the language rather than two spellings of one. `String(n)` calls the global;
-- `new Error(m)` constructs it. A relation that merged them could not state the
-- audit below.
es_static_call[code](C, Name, Key, Rel) :- callee_of[code](C, N),
                                           es_static[code](N, Name, Key, Rel).
es_global_invoke[code](C, Name, Rel)    :- callee_of[code](C, N),
                                           es_global[code](N, Name, Rel, _).
es_global_construct[code](X, Name, Rel) :- transfer_site[code](X, new_expression),
                                           ast_child[code](X, callee, 0, N),
                                           es_global[code](N, Name, Rel, _).

-- ...AND THE FORM USED RATHER THAN RECORDED. `new Math()` is a TypeError and
-- the model can now say WHY: `Math` is a `namespace_object`, TypeScript
-- declares no `MathConstructor` behind it, and only a `constructor_binding` has
-- one. This is the same class of claim as `ambiguous_call[audit]` reporting a
-- static reached through an instance — a runtime error visible statically.
edb(constructible_form).
constructible_form(constructor_binding).
es_construct_not_constructor[audit](X, Name, Form) :- es_global_construct[code](X, Name, _),
                                                      lib_global(Name, _, Form),
                                                      not constructible_form(Form).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 5. THE ERA QUESTION, IN THE SAME WORDS AS EVERY OTHER AXIS.
--
-- `lib_unsupported[audit]` asks it of a prototype method and `unsupported[audit]`
-- of a syntax feature; these ask it of a binding and of a static, on the same
-- scale, through the same `reaches[audit]` composition. `Object.hasOwn` is
-- es2022 and `Promise.allSettled` es2020, so a corpus calling them is not valid
-- under es2017 — and until now the model could say that about `??=` and about
-- `.at`, and not about `Object.entries`.
--
-- LIKE rules/js-env-api.rofl THIS PACK NEEDS TWO WORLDS. `selects[flow]` and
-- `callee_of[code]` come from the call-graph world; `environment` and
-- `reaches[audit]` from facts/js-env.rofl, which that world does not load. A
-- world with only one half derives the first four sections and reports
-- `environment` as UNPOPULATABLE — the kernel saying "you brought half a
-- question", which is the right thing for it to say.
es_global_unsupported[audit](E, X, Name) :- es_global[code](X, Name, Rel, _),
                                            environment(E), not reaches[audit](E, Rel).
es_static_unsupported[audit](E, N, Name, Key) :- es_static[code](N, Name, Key, Rel),
                                                 environment(E), not reaches[audit](E, Rel).
